WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.730
Why does that bearded fellow who plays Charles
Goodnight on "1883" look like someone we've

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:05.730
seen before?

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.010
And what does he have to do with the show
behind the scenes?

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.770
Keep watching for the answer.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:14.170
Even people only vaguely aware of shows like
"Yellowstone" and "1883" probably know Taylor

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:18.189
Sheridan has something to do with the massively
successful franchise at the heart of the Paramount

00:00:18.189 --> 00:00:19.189
Network.

00:00:19.189 --> 00:00:20.189
And he does.

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:21.189
He's the creator.

00:00:21.189 --> 00:00:24.249
"Yellowstone," the first TV series Sheridan
has created, is an enduring hit, spawning

00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:28.599
multiple spinoffs, including: "1883," the
series that tells the story of the first Duttons

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:33.170
in Montana; "6666," an upcoming series set
at the real-life Four Sixes ranch in Texas;

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:37.100
and "1932," about the Duttons' difficulties
during the Great Depression.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.100
Sheridan is also the creator of "Mayor of
Kingstown," a Paramount+ series starring Jeremy

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.000
Renner, and a slew of other upcoming originals,
including "Tulsa King," starring Sylvester

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.450
Stallone; "Lioness," starring Zoe Saldana;
"Land Man," starring Billy Bob Thornton; and

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.469
"Bass Reeves," starring David Oyelowo.

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:55.160
While Sheridan is arguably best known for
his behind-the-scenes work on these series,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.739
his career in Hollywood goes back much further
than the launch of "Yellowstone" in 2018.

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:03.270
Before Sheridan made a name for himself as
a writer, he primarily worked as an actor,

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.330
and he's been in some pretty recognizable
roles.

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:09.240
In recent years, Sheridan has focused on writing,
but he's also made a handful of appearances

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.960
on his own projects, including a brief role
in 2016's "Hell or High Water" and an appearance

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.010
in "1883" as Charles Goodnight, a real-life
historical figure.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:20.190
"When you bite off more than you can chew,
you sure bite big."

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:24.020
Well before he led the "Yellowstone" franchise,
Sheridan appeared as Deputy Chief David Hale

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.720
on a total of 21 episodes of FX's hit series
"Sons of Anarchy."

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.360
He was an integral part of the early series,
appearing in all but four episodes of the

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.500
first two seasons.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.350
Throughout the first season, Hale operates
as one of the last honest officers in Charming.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:40.220
Hale hates SAMCRO's criminal enterprise, so
he initially pursues the group in a fashion

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.940
decidedly different from that of his fellow
law enforcement officers, most of whom have

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.340
essentially learned to live with the biker
gang's actions.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:49.470
"You make a deal with the devil to keep the
peace.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.680
But look at this.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.890
Look at what we let happen."

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:58.710
In Season 2, Hale begins to see the wisdom
in working with the group and opts to form

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.340
an alliance of sorts with Charlie Hunnam's
Jax Teller.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.410
Sadly, their alliance is relatively short-lived.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:07.670
In the Season 3 premiere, Hale is killed in
a drive-by shooting at a funeral.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.490
In the years since his exit from the acclaimed
FX series, Sheridan has spoken rather bluntly

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:12.900
about his decision to leave the show.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.380
In a wide-ranging interview with Deadline
in 2021, Sheridan admitted that he was troubled

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.440
with a number of things on "Sons of Anarchy,"
including his pay.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.130
However, even more troubling to Sheridan was
the way that he was viewed as a lower-level

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.630
team member on the production.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:26.630
He said,

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:27.630
"It wasn't so much over money.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:29.620
It was so much more that that's how the business
saw me."

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:33.050
Sheridan's departure motivated him to pick
up writing again, which would lead to big

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:34.050
things.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:37.700
Sheridan isn't exactly shy about including
himself as an on-screen presence in the series

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:38.920
that he leads.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.819
Sheridan made his first appearance on "Yellowstone"
in Season 1's "Coming Home" as Travis Wheatley,

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:45.440
a role which he has reprised a number of times
since.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.580
A horse trader, Travis is a through-and-through
cowboy, known for his no-nonsense attitude

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:50.970
and expert riding abilities.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.069
While Sheridan only appeared in a single episode
of Season 1, he returned as Travis for two

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:56.630
more episodes in Season 2.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:00.540
He opted to sit out Season 3, but made up
for his lack of screen time by appearing in

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.730
five different episodes during Season 4.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.830
As a favor to Kevin Costner's John Dutton,
Travis gives Jefferson White's Jimmy Hurdstrom

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:10.200
a ride to the "6666" ranch in Texas, the cowboy's
home state.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.200
"That's all cowboyin' is, all right?

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.400
It's you and a horse, doin' a job, tryin'
like hell to no let the other down."

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.170
We think it's safe to say that Travis will
probably find another reason to visit Montana

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.910
in "Yellowstone" Season 5, too.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:26.170
And, while not much is known about the "6666"
spinoff yet, it also seems like a safe bet

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:30.120
that any "Yellowstone" series is bound to
feature Sheridan in some role.

